s or diseases with complex etiology often require multiple treatments, each addressing one specific aspect of the disease. Such treatments are typically administered sequentially in an integrated fashion. For example, in alcohol-associated hepatitis (AH), an acute liver disease caused by excessive alcohol drinking, successful treatment requires therapies that reduce liver inflammation during the acute phase, followed by interventions that address the underlying alcohol use disorder (AUD) to achieve optimal outcomes. Selecting an optimal treatment combination, however, presents great challenges in study design due to the involvement of multiple drug combinations and treatment phases. In this paper, we describe a new trial design based on a Bayesian model

